WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.112
Quantization: fewer bits per weight.

2
00:00:02.392 --> 00:00:15.192
Models are usually trained and published in 16-bit floating point (FP16 or BF16): 2 bytes per parameter, so an 8B model needs about 16 GB just for weights.

3
00:00:15.472 --> 00:00:25.435
Quantization stores the weights with fewer bits, typically 8, 6, 5, or 4, and converts them back to higher precision on the fly during computation.

4
00:00:25.715 --> 00:00:27.720
Modern schemes quantize in blocks.

5
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:40.992
A group of weights (for example 32, 64, or 256 of them) shares a scale, and sometimes an offset, stored at higher precision, so each block can use its small range of integers well.

6
00:00:41.272 --> 00:00:47.288
Those scales cost bits, which is why a “4-bit” format has more than 4 effective bits per weight.

7
00:00:47.568 --> 00:00:48.336
llama.

8
00:00:48.616 --> 00:00:52.136
cpp’s quantize README measures this on Llama 3.

9
00:00:52.416 --> 00:00:55.275
1 8B: Q4_K_M comes to 4.

10
00:00:55.555 --> 00:00:57.347
89 bits per weight (4.

11
00:00:57.627 --> 00:01:00.677
58 GiB), Q5_K_M to 5.

12
00:01:00.957 --> 00:01:02.877
70, Q6_K to 6.

13
00:01:03.157 --> 00:01:05.205
56, Q8_0 to 8.

14
00:01:05.485 --> 00:01:07.661
50, and F16 to 16.

15
00:01:07.941 --> 00:01:09.179
0 (14.

16
00:01:09.459 --> 00:01:10.867
96 GiB).

17
00:01:11.147 --> 00:01:16.139
The “K_M” variants are mixes that keep some sensitive tensors at higher precision.

18
00:01:16.419 --> 00:01:22.200
MLX’s converter defaults to 4-bit affine quantization with a group size of 64.

19
00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:23.547
What do you give up?

20
00:01:23.827 --> 00:01:35.923
The same README says quality loss is usually measured as perplexity (how surprised the model is by reference text) or KL divergence from the unquantized model’s output distribution.

21
00:01:36.203 --> 00:01:38.400
The research gives a consistent picture.

22
00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:58.456
Dettmers and Zettlemoyer (2022) ran more than 35,000 experiments across model families and found that, for a fixed memory budget, 4-bit precision was almost universally the best trade-off between total model bits and zero-shot accuracy, with small block sizes helping.

23
00:01:58.736 --> 00:02:03.493
In other words, a bigger model at 4 bits usually beats a smaller one at 8.

24
00:02:03.773 --> 00:02:13.821
Below 4 bits, things degrade: Huang and colleagues (2024) found Llama 3 suffered non-negligible degradation at very low bit widths.

25
00:02:14.101 --> 00:02:16.981
Methods such as GPTQ and LLM.

26
00:02:17.261 --> 00:02:21.955
int8() (both in the sources) are different ways of quantizing with less damage.

27
00:02:22.235 --> 00:02:26.416
The engineering rule: 4 to 6 bits is the usual starting range.

28
00:02:26.696 --> 00:02:39.581
Treat anything lower as an experiment, and measure on your own task, because averages over benchmarks can hide a sharp drop on exactly the thing you need, such as precise extraction or long-context retrieval.
